    <div id=3D"container-9c2531eaef" class=3D"cmp-container">
       =20
        <div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pl-0 pr-0 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-Grid=
Column--phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large cmp-di-text-bullets">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <p><b>Are foundation models reaching a plateau?</b> Foundation mode=
ls=E2=80=94large AI systems trained on massive data sets=E2=80=94face a cri=
tical question: Will they keep improving exponentially, or will their capab=
ilities plateau? New models are still improving, but some metrics show they=
=E2=80=99ve not delivered the dramatic performance leaps seen in earlier ge=
nerations.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/te=
chnology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-1=
" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 1">1</a></sup> Plus, bigger model=
s drive up energy consumption and computing costs. New scaling approaches, =
like techniques that allow models more time to process complex problems,<su=
p><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-mana=
gement/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-2" aria-label=
=3D"Endnotes reference number 2">2</a></sup> could shift us away from =E2=
=80=9Cbigger model =3D better performance.=E2=80=9D This means current mode=
ls can improve by optimizing prompting and implementation strategies. How w=
ell businesses deploy, fine-tune, and integrate AI into redesigned processe=
s will likely matter more than having the latest foundation model.</p>
<p><b>New data &gt; synthetic data &gt; old data.</b> As foundation models =
train on similar publicly available data sets, data itself stops being a co=
mpetitive advantage. Old data loses value as the world changes. Synthetic d=
ata=E2=80=94AI-generated content used to train other AI=E2=80=94helps fill =
gaps, with predictions that 80% of data used by AI tools will be synthetic =
by 2028, up from 20% in 2024.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en=
/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-sig=
nals.html#endnote-3" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 3">3</a></sup>=
 However, it has a performance ceiling, typically achieving 90% to 95% of r=
eal data quality.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/to=
pics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#en=
dnote-4" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 4">4</a></sup> Worse, AI t=
rained primarily on AI-generated content can create model collapse, a degen=
erative process where models lose information about rare patterns, confuse =
concepts, and eventually produce bland, repetitive outputs.<sup><a href=3D"=
https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-t=
rends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-5" aria-label=3D"Endnotes r=
eference number 5">5</a></sup> Those with access to fresh information=E2=80=
=94real-time user interactions, proprietary business data, breaking researc=
h=E2=80=94have the advantage. Translation: Companies controlling the intera=
ction layer (search engines, social platforms, AI assistants, smart devices=
) win.</p>
<p><b>Neuromorphic chips supercharge computing.</b> Neuromorphic chips are =
brain-inspired processors that are more energy-efficient than traditional g=
raphics processing units (GPUs) for certain AI tasks. GPUs have separate ar=
eas for memory and processing, while neuromorphic chips combine both in the=
 same place. Neuromorphic chips are event-driven=E2=80=94they only process =
information when something happens=E2=80=94while GPUs run constantly at ful=
l speed. This means neuromorphic chips can use 80 to 100 times less energy =
for AI tasks involving sporadic signals, like analyzing sensor data or proc=
essing information in autonomous vehicles, though GPUs remain superior for =
continuous, high-throughput computation.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitt=
e.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-tec=
hnology-signals.html#endnote-6" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 6">=
6</a></sup> As AI moves from data centers to billions of edge devices (see =
next signal), the energy efficiency advantage becomes critical. Widespread =
adoption of neuromorphic computing is expected by 2030.<sup><a href=3D"http=
s://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trend=
s/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-7" aria-label=3D"Endnotes refer=
ence number 7">7</a></sup></p>
<p><b>The rise of edge AI and on-device processing.</b> Instead of sending =
data to distant cloud servers, edge AI runs directly on devices=E2=80=94you=
r phone, smartwatch, security camera, or industrial robot. Why it matters: =
latency (autonomous vehicles can=E2=80=99t wait for server responses), priv=
acy (data never leaves your device), exploding costs (cloud bills reaching =
tens of millions monthly), and internet dependency. Edge AI=E2=80=99s poten=
tial is reflected in the market for generative AI=E2=80=93capable smartphon=
es, which grew nearly 364% year over year in 2024 to 234.2 million units so=
ld annually, and heading toward 912 million by 2028.<sup><a href=3D"https:/=
/www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2=
026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-8" aria-label=3D"Endnotes referenc=
e number 8">8</a></sup> Real-world applications include smart cameras doing=
 real-time recognition locally, industrial sensors predicting equipment fai=
lures, and health wearables monitoring vitals without broadcasting medical =
data. This is a fundamental shift already in motion.</p>
<p><b>Will AI-native personal devices and wearables become mainstream?</b> =
Companies are experimenting with AI-native wearable devices beyond smartpho=
nes=E2=80=94pendants that record and transcribe conversations, smart glasse=
s with real-time translation, and screenless pins with voice interaction. T=
he global wearable technology market is projected to reach US$265.4 billion=
 by 2026, and tech giants are investing heavily in next-generation form fac=
tors.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technol=
ogy-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-9" ari=
a-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 9">9</a></sup> However, market adoptio=
n remains deeply uncertain, and the landscape is littered with failed glass=
es, pins, and other wearable or pocket-sized form factors.<sup><a href=3D"h=
ttps://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-tr=
ends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-10" aria-label=3D"Endnotes r=
eference number 10">10</a></sup> Questions persist about whether consumers =
actually want separate AI devices or prefer AI integrated into the phones a=
nd earbuds they already use. The winning form factor=E2=80=94if one emerges=
 at all=E2=80=94remains undetermined, with success dependent on addressing =
privacy concerns and delivering functionality that justifies carrying an ad=
ditional device.</p>
<p><b>Biometric authentication as next-level cybersecurity.</b> Because AI =
can replicate voices, forge documents, and mimic behavioral patterns, biome=
tric authentication is becoming critical for verifying physical presence an=
d identity. As deepfakes and AI-powered fraud grow more sophisticated, orga=
nizations are rapidly adopting biometric systems: In one study of chief inf=
ormation security officers, 92% of those surveyed said they have already im=
plemented, are implementing, or plan to implement passwordless authenticati=
on.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technolog=
y-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-11" aria=
-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 11">11</a></sup> However, biometrics wo=
n=E2=80=99t be the sole solution. Compromised biometric data cannot be chan=
ged like a password, and privacy concerns remain significant. The future po=
ints toward hybrid approaches where biometrics serve as the primary but not=
 exclusive verification method.</p>
<p><b>The AI agent privacy trade-off.</b> Truly capable personal AI assista=
nts require unprecedented access to personal data=E2=80=94and that access i=
s already being granted. To book restaurants, manage schedules, or filter e=
mails effectively, personal AI agents need years of message history, calend=
ar entries, browsing data, stored passwords, credit card information, and i=
ntimate personal preferences.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en=
/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-sig=
nals.html#endnote-12" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 12">12</a></s=
up> But the trade-off is stark: Once personal data is incorporated into AI =
models, the right to erasure becomes nearly impossible.<sup><a href=3D"http=
s://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trend=
s/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-13" aria-label=3D"Endnotes refe=
rence number 13">13</a></sup> And of course, security concerns are signific=
ant. The public response is already splitting: Some eagerly grant permissio=
ns for capability while others resist. But the consent paradox remains. Use=
rs must grant extensive permissions to make AI assistants useful, but most =
don=E2=80=99t fully understand the scope or permanence of what they=E2=80=
=99re agreeing to share.</p>
<p><b>GEO overtakes SEO.</b> Users are increasingly turning to AI chatbots =
over traditional search engines. The race is on to appear in AI-generated a=
nswers=E2=80=94a shift from search engine optimization (SEO) to generative =
engine optimization (GEO). AI-generated answers already dominate search res=
ults across major search engines, reducing click-through rates to conventio=
nal websites by more than a third.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/=
us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technolog=
y-signals.html#endnote-14" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 14">14</=
a></sup> AI platforms now drive 6.5% of organic traffic, projected to hit 1=
4.5% within a year.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/=
topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#=
endnote-15" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 15">15</a></sup> GEO di=
ffers fundamentally from SEO, prioritizing semantic richness over keywords,=
 author expertise over backlinks, and being cited in AI responses over page=
 views.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/techn=
ology-management/tech-trends/2026/2026-technology-signals.html#endnote-16" =
aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 16">16</a></sup> Just as paid searc=
h defined the 2000s and social media advertising dominated the 2010s, AI-ge=
nerated responses are becoming the most critical marketing channel of the 2=
020s.</p>
<p>Some of these signals may mature into dominant forces. Others may fade. =
But all of them reflect the same underlying reality: The pace of technologi=
cal change has fundamentally shifted. But the speed of adaptation matters m=
ore than the certainty of prediction. The organizations that thrive won=E2=
=80=99t be those that predict which signals become trends; they=E2=80=99ll =
be those that build the capacity to sense, evaluate, and respond quickly to=
 what emerges. Those that wait for clarity will find themselves adapting to=
 changes their competitors are already leveraging.</p>

    </div>
</div>

